1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:07,533
Put an arrow at every point of a grid. Neighbouring arrows like to point the same way.

2
00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:17,033
Now shrink the grid to nothing. Do the arrows become a smooth quantum field, and does that field have a mass gap?

3
00:00:17,033 --> 00:00:28,400
In October 2026, OpenAI claimed to answer both questions with a proof, for a famous stand-in for the Yang–Mills problem.

4
00:00:34,633 --> 00:00:40,567
In quantum field theory, particles are ripples in fields that fill space.

5
00:00:40,567 --> 00:00:47,600
The strong nuclear force is described by a Yang–Mills theory.

6
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:54,133
Its force carriers have no mass in the equations, yet the lightest particles it makes do.

7
00:00:54,133 --> 00:01:00,967
The smallest energy above the empty vacuum is the mass gap.

8
00:01:00,967 --> 00:01:12,200
The Millennium problem asks for both: build four-dimensional Yang–Mills theory with full rigor, and prove that it has a mass gap.

9
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:18,700
No interacting quantum field theory in four dimensions has ever been built rigorously.

10
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:24,167
So physicists practise on simpler models.

11
00:01:24,167 --> 00:01:33,800
So: for Yang–Mills, build the theory and prove the gap. Both are open.

12
00:01:38,033 --> 00:01:49,133
In the O(3) model, each point of a square grid carries an arrow of length one, pointing anywhere in three-dimensional space.

13
00:01:49,133 --> 00:01:53,300
A number called beta sets how strongly neighbours align.

14
00:01:53,300 --> 00:02:01,700
These are samples from our own simulation: larger beta, larger patches.

15
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:10,200
Like Yang–Mills, this model is asymptotically free, as Polyakov showed in 1975,

16
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:20,500
and it is expected to make a mass gap out of nothing. But it has only two dimensions, and no gauge field.

17
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:25,733
In two dimensions the arrows never line up across the whole grid.

18
00:02:25,733 --> 00:02:30,067
The question is how fast distant arrows forget each other.

19
00:02:30,067 --> 00:02:38,000
Our simulation shows exponential decay: straight lines on this log scale.

20
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:47,933
So: a grid of arrows, asymptotically free, and expected to have a gap.

21
00:02:52,166 --> 00:03:02,633
The decay length, called the correlation length, explodes as beta grows: roughly like e to the two pi beta.

22
00:03:02,633 --> 00:03:13,633
To reach a continuum, shrink the grid while raising beta, so that the correlation length stays fixed in physical units.

23
00:03:13,633 --> 00:03:21,900
The paper measures everything in units set by the lattice itself: the correlation length and the susceptibility.

24
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:30,133
In those units, our simulations at very different beta fall on one curve.

25
00:03:30,133 --> 00:03:40,733
Proving that such a limit really exists, for every correlation, and finding out what it is, is the hard part.

26
00:03:40,733 --> 00:03:50,100
So: raise beta, shrink the grid, and keep the physical scale fixed.

27
00:03:54,333 --> 00:04:05,367
Here is the claim. As beta goes to infinity, in any way at all, the rescaled fields converge to one limit.

28
00:04:05,367 --> 00:04:09,633
The limit satisfies the Osterwalder–Schrader axioms.

29
00:04:09,633 --> 00:04:20,000
So it defines a genuine relativistic quantum field theory, in one space and one time dimension.

30
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:30,100
It has a single vacuum, and every other state has energy at least some positive m: a mass gap.

31
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:37,967
And it is interacting: a four-point correlation shows it is not a free field.

32
00:04:37,967 --> 00:04:47,667
So the claim is one canonical limit: an interacting quantum field theory with a mass gap.

33
00:04:51,900 --> 00:05:03,667
The method is the renormalization group. Group the arrows into squares, keep one averaged arrow per square, and repeat.

34
00:05:03,667 --> 00:05:08,234
The paper does this exactly, tracking every correction.

35
00:05:08,234 --> 00:05:17,567
New estimates show the corrections shrink at each step, and rare rough regions stay under control.

36
00:05:17,567 --> 00:05:20,100
A square grid is not round.

37
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:31,134
Comparing two tilted blockings gives a rotation by an irrational angle, and with it full rotation symmetry in the limit.

38
00:05:31,134 --> 00:05:40,167
Finally, the Osterwalder–Schrader reconstruction turns the decay of correlations into the mass gap.

39
00:05:40,167 --> 00:05:48,634
So: block, control the corrections, and reconstruct.

40
00:05:52,866 --> 00:06:04,933
It comes with companion papers, including one claiming exponential decay at every beta, for all O(n) models with n at least three.

41
00:06:04,933 --> 00:06:08,700
Only that companion result is checked in Lean.

42
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:17,733
The continuum limit itself is not formalized, and we found no expert assessment yet.

43
00:06:17,733 --> 00:06:27,166
It does not solve the Yang–Mills problem, which needs four dimensions and a gauge field.

44
00:06:27,166 --> 00:06:38,700
But if it holds, a model physicists have studied for fifty years finally has a rigorous continuum theory with a mass gap.
